      ":question/id": "1c261405-1fb7-5f8f-992e-8a8c19696484",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-18052",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/0d/0d92a4d7b9c59b0d6bc8ffec9926bcf189c9bbe808c5bed7a02d6ab09bcebcd0.png)\n\nLet $P(-2, 2)$ and $Q(3, 3)$. Find $\\mid PQ^(→) \\mid$."
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186233366,
      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
      },
      ":question/id": "02dcce11-c60f-5753-ba12-d8ce046f2ae1",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-125118",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/f3/f35a3ad730621023ec86e5727588fc86083ee500b3a72c55ab2808bd290019e9.png)\n\nLet $P(1,2)$ and $Q(-2,1).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=i+2j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=-2i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =(-2i+j)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =-3i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186233596,
      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
      },
      ":question/id": "f6f7008d-b645-50b0-a00b-9cf227ed2ff3",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-18049",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/1a/1a250499768a91e3e225a05d141e42d7ed89168ba82c5560777f64b8b29fac4a.png)\n\nLet $C(-1,3)$ and $D(1,-2).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{CD}|.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=-i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} \\\\  & =d-c \\\\  & =(i-2j)-(-i+3j) \\\\  & =2i-5j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{CD}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186233598,
      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
      },
      ":question/id": "c14ed0ed-41e3-5534-b77e-503efe93d887",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-18055",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/1e/1e3e9c336bf85cf0f19b2326f12c5fd986fee5b0aae65b8d79257ea234efee13.png)\n\nPoints $P$ and $Q$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "From the picture, we obtain $P(1,1)$ and $Q(2,3).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=i+j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-(i+j) \\\\  & =i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
  ],
  ":knowledge-point/title": "Calculating a Vector From Position Vectors and then Computing its Magnitude"
